              "**Yes. That is a fair assessment: a distinct, useful auxiliary framework, with concrete progress and some initial promise as an additional tool for studying collisions.**\n\nThe distinction is mathematical. The **de Bruijn–Newman flow changes the physical function as Newman time varies**, moving its zeros. Our **auxiliary flow evolves a spatial density at fixed physical parameters**, preserving the relevant function value and derivative as integrals. It connects the collision condition to concentration and finite-time breakdown in a different evolution.\n\nWithin the current written proofs, that framework has already provided:\n\n- **A quantitative collision consequence:** a simultaneous zero forces breakdown by an explicit auxiliary-time deadline.\n- **Tools for analysing that evolution:** gradient identities, residual equations, coupled energy estimates, and local existence bounds.\n- **Demonstrable improvements:** radial geometry and, now, coupling involving the complete theta coefficients produce strictly sharper bounds.\n\nThat last point supports some optimism: the framework is yielding estimates that improve when we retain more of the structure.\n\nThe remaining distinction is between **having useful analytical tools** and **having obtained a new restriction on physical collisions**. We have made progress on the former. The latter still requires an estimate that exploits theta’s actual evolution strongly enough to distinguish it from the colliding controls. The latest proof also awaits its planned assessment.\n\nSo yes—“initial promise for providing additional tools to study collisions” is appropriately measured. It recognizes what has been accomplished while leaving the decisive mathematical test open."
            ]
          },
